  "The stored chain holds no zero-bid lines (two-sided capture), so Cboe's two-consecutive-zero-bid stop cannot trigger: every stored OTM strike with a bid enters the strip, which can only add variance relative to Cboe's rule on the same book; the stored book's own strike range is the binding limit.",
  "No quote time is stored for this snapshot (Yahoo); the calculation uses 16:00 ET on the session and the official close is the 16:15 ET print (the official index traded 15.72-22.31 that session). Any intraday drift between the snapshot and 16:15 is unmeasured.",
  "The first Cboe candidate expiry after 30 days (2026-11-20) is not in the stored chain (the bot stores calibration expiries and neighbours), so the bracket is 2026-11-13 (28.0d) - 2026-12-04 (49.0d) instead of a <= 7-day bracket.",
  "near-term 2026-11-13: 110 puts / 84 calls, strip K/F 0.469-1.120",
  "next-term 2026-12-04: 54 puts / 56 calls, strip K/F 0.442-1.170; K0 fell back to the nearest lower strike with both sides quoted",
  "Model variance swaps at 30 days (k in [-3, 2], wings beyond the fitted K/F 0.70-1.30 extrapolated, so each inherits its model's wing bias): SVI-cal 18.98, SVI 19.06, eSSVI 17.81.",
  "Gap attribution (VIX, SVI-cal surface): replicated - official = -0.29. Strikes beyond the stored strip would add +0.04 under the surface's wing; the stored quotes beyond the fitted K/F 0.70-1.30 are 0.36 index points richer than that wing, so the truncation figure likely understates. Inside the fitted range market and surface differ by +0.20. Left unexplained by the chain: +0.24 (official - replicated - truncation): snapshot timing vs the 16:15 ET print, stale quotes, rate."
